PipeGuard BD: An Integrated Pipeline Sensing, Repair and Rescue System for Bangladesh
Project abstract, technical overview, and business plan
Prepared by: Raian Rishad
Date: November 2026
Status: Prototype-stage concept. Figures marked as assumptions or illustrative must be replaced with measured data and supplier quotes before use in a formal proposal.
Abstract
Bangladesh loses large amounts of water and gas every year through damaged, ageing, and poorly monitored pipelines. Sewer and septic workers continue to die from toxic gas inside confined spaces. Existing practice is reactive: a leak is found when pressure drops, a road floods, or a citizen complains, and repair means digging up the street. PipeGuard BD is an integrated system that makes pipeline care proactive. It has four connected components. (1) A Distributed Acoustic Sensing (DAS) prototype listens to vibration along the pipeline, measures its frequency and position, and classifies the event (small leak, crack, digging or impact, blockage, normal flow). (2) A material-specific self-healing repair library matches each pipe material (PVC, HDPE, steel and cast iron, concrete) and each fluid (water, sewage, gas) with a suitable sealing chemistry, such as superabsorbent polymer wraps, a fibre–bentonite–sodium silicate in-fluid sealant, moisture-curing polyurethane and epoxy systems, and microbial calcium carbonate for concrete. (3) A ground-level rover with a 6-DOF robotic arm and IP camera drives to the DAS-reported position, pinpoints the defect, marks it for a small excavation, delivers repair material through access points, and verifies the repair. (4) A small in-pipe robot crawls through small pipes to inspect, map defects, apply local sealant, and assist rescue or retrieval where something or someone is trapped. A web platform ties these together with a risk heat map, a repair-recommendation engine, and a citizen complaint portal routed to WASA and gas distribution companies. The business model serves government (B2G) and industry (B2B) through monitoring-as-a-service, repair-as-a-service, consumables, and performance-based contracts. Public reports show Dhaka WASA losing roughly 22% of its water and Bangladesh's gas distribution losing about 6.28% of gas worth about Tk 3,790 crore in FY2023-24, so even a modest recovery justifies a pilot.
Keywords: pipeline safety, distributed acoustic sensing, self-healing materials, leak repair, in-pipe robot, non-revenue water, gas loss, public–private partnership, Bangladesh
