Why Bangladesh needs a local solution.
Dense cities, old networks. Water, sewage and gas lines run under crowded streets, so each excavation causes traffic jams, business disruption and damage to neighbouring utilities.
Contamination risk. Where sewage and water pipes run close together, a leak can draw contaminated water into the supply, which is a public health risk.
Gas security. As domestic gas declines and imported LNG becomes more important, each cubic metre lost costs more.
Worker safety. Manual entry into sewers and septic tanks kills workers every year. A system that inspects and tests the air before anyone enters saves lives.
Weak feedback loop. Citizens report low gas pressure, sewage overflow, and dirty water, but complaints rarely link to sensor evidence. PipeGuard BD connects them.
2. How the whole system works
Layered architecture: Sense (DAS) → Decide (software and recommendation engine) → Pinpoint and repair from outside (rover) → Inspect and repair from inside (in-pipe robot) → Verify and report (DAS plus platform).
End-to-end workflow
Continuous listening. The DAS unit monitors vibration along the pipeline and learns the normal acoustic signature of each segment.
Detection. An unusual signature raises an event with a position (distance along the pipe), dominant frequency (Hz), energy level, and confidence.
Classification and severity. Rules and, later, machine learning label the event (leak, crack, digging or impact, blockage) and set a severity level.
Recommendation. The platform looks up the pipe record (material, diameter, age, fluid, pressure) and recommends a repair method and an estimated quantity of material.
Citizen cross-check. Nearby citizen complaints (low pressure, wet road, bad smell) raise the priority and confirm the event.
Rover response. The rover drives to the reported position, uses its camera and ground sensors to pinpoint the defect, marks the spot,t and sends coordinates and photos to the control centre.
Repair. Depending on the case, the rover's arm applies an external wrap or patch, or doses sealant through a hydrant, valve, or manhole so that the flow carries it to the leak. Where the damage is inside a small pipe, the in-pipe robot is deployed through an access point to inspect and seal it.
Verification and record. DAS listens again. If the leak signature has gone, the platform stores a repair record with time, location, material used,d and before-and-after signals. If not, the event is escalated to a human crew.
Safety rule for gas lines: the system never attempts autonomous sealing on gas pipes. It detects, marks, alerts the gas company and the public-safety line, and leaves the repair to certified crews.
3. Component 1: DAS prototype
Principle. In a DAS system, a laser pulse travels along an optical fibre. A tiny fraction of the light scatters back (Rayleigh backscatter). When the fibre is shaken by vibration, the backscatter changes. The time of arrival gives the position along the fibre, and the change in the signal gives the amplitude and frequency of the vibration. One fibre therefore acts as thousands of virtual microphones. Commercial systems typically resolve position to metres over long distances.
What the prototype measures and shows
Vibration waveform and frequency spectrum (via the Fast Fourier Transform, FFT).
Position of the event along the pipe.
Event type: small leak (continuous broadband noise from a fluid jet), crack, third-party digging or impact (low-frequency impulsive bursts), blockage or flow change, normal.
Severity and confidence score.
Processing chain. Band-pass filtering → FFT → feature extraction (dominant frequency, band energy, bursts per minute, spatial spread) → rule-based classification with adjustable thresholds → later, a trained model using real field recordings. The thresholds in the prototype are illustrative until calibrated on real pipes.
Installation options. Fibre laid alongside new pipes during construction is the cheapest. For existing pipes, fibre can be laid in the same trench during planned works, or accelerometer and hydrophone nodes can be placed at valves and manholes as a lower-cost retrofit.
Limits. Noise from traffic and construction can mask leaks, plastic pipes carry sound over shorter distances than metal ones, and the system needs calibration for each network.
