Core chemistry (summary)
Superabsorbent polymer: sodium polyacrylate, –[CH₂–CH(COONa)]ₙ–, made from acrylic acid (C₃H₄O₂) neutralised with NaOH and cross-linked with N,N′-methylenebisacrylamide (C₇H₁₀N₂O₂). In water: –COONa + H₂O ⇌ –COO⁻ + Na⁺. The charged chains repel, and osmotic pressure pulls water in, so the polymer swells into a gel that plugs the hole. Indicative swelling is 100 to 300 g/g in tap water and only 30 to 70 g/g in salty water.
Sodium silicate binder: Na₂SiO₃ + Ca²⁺ → CaSiO₃↓ + 2Na⁺, then polycondensation of Si(OH)₄ to a silica gel. Together with cellulose fibre (C₆H₁₀O₅)ₙ and sodium bentonite clay, it forms a plug that bridges holes of roughly 0.5 to 2 mm.
Moisture-curing polyurethane: R–N=C=O + H₂O → R–NH₂ + CO₂↑, then R–NH₂ + R′–N=C=O → R–NH–CO–NH–R′ (urea linkage). It hardens on contact with water.
Epoxy–amine: the epoxide ring of DGEBA (C₂₁H₂₄O₄) opens on reaction with an amine hardener to form a cross-linked network.
Microbial calcium carbonate (concrete): Ca(C₃H₅O₃)₂ + 6O₂ → CaCO₃ + 5CO₂ + 5H₂O, then CO₂ + Ca(OH)₂ → CaCO₃↓ + H₂O.
Leak physics used to size the repair. Leak rate follows Q = Cₑ·A·√(2ΔP/ρ), with Cₑ about 0.61 for a sharp-edged hole. A 1 mm hole at about 100 kPa leaks roughly 407 mL/min, which is about 586 litres per day. Doubling the hole diameter quadruples the leak, so early sealing of small defects is the most valuable.
Honest limits. The gel and in-fluid sealants suit small holes and are partly temporary. The sealant formulations must be validated on a test rig, and any material touching drinking water must be certified for potable use (for example under NSF/ANSI 61 or an equivalent standard).
5. Component 3: Surface rover with 6-DOF arm (outer-side monitoring and repair)
Hardware. Rover chassis with encoders, GPS and IMU; 6-DOF robotic arm; IP camera; ground microphone or vibration sensor; methane and hydrogen sulphide sensors; metered dosing pump with swappable tool heads (wrap applicator, dosing hose connector, marking sprayer).
Operating modes
Patrol and monitor. Drives along the pipeline route, taking camera, gas,s and moisture readings.
Pinpoint. Goes to the DAS position (accurate to a few metres) and uses the arm to press the sensor on the ground at several points. The loudest point is the defect, which narrows the search area for the excavation crew.
Mark and report. Marks the spot, records GPS coordinates and photos, and issues an excavation ticket. The goal is "dig small, dig once".
Repair from outside. Applies an external wrap or patch on exposed pipe sections, or connects a dosing hose to a hydrant, valve, ve or manhole and injects sealant upstream so the flow carries it to the leak.
Manhole probe. Lowers the camera and gas sensors on a cable into a manhole to inspect a sewer and test the air before any worker enters.
Verify. Triggers a DAS re-scan and records the result.
Safety and limits. The rover works from ground level and cannot enter pipes. Ordinary motors and electronics can spark, so it must not operate inside a suspected gas-leak zone unless it is built to explosion-protection standards. It stays at a safe distance, detects, and alerts.
6. Component 4: Small in-pipe monitoring and repair robot
Design target. A tethered crawler (tracked or wheeled) sized for small pipes, with a tether-length counter for position, LED lighting and camera, and a sealed body. Target pipe diameters should be fixed by the first design and tested on a PVC rig.
Functions
Inspection and mapping: records video and builds a defect map (cracks, joint displacement, corrosion, root intrusion, debris).
Air and condition sensing: oxygen, hydrogen sulphide, methane, humidity, and pressure, so crews know the air is unsafe before entering.
Local repair: a small dispensing nozzle applies polyurethane or epoxy sealant to a located crack or joint, and the DAS re-scan verifies it.
Blockage and object location: finds blockages and stuck objects (tools, cables, valuables) and, with an optional hook or gripper, retrieves light items.
Rescue assist: in pipes, culverts and drains large enough for a person, it acts as the first eyes and voice for the rescue team, with camera, two-way audio, gas readings and the ability to carry a small payload such as a light or radio. It supports, and does not replace, the fire service and trained rescuers.
Limits. It is for sewage, stormwater,r and water lines. It is not intended for live gas lines. A very small pipe cannot hold a person, so the rescue role applies only to larger conduits, while in small pipes the focus is inspection, repair, and retrieval.
7. Software platform and citizen portal
Engineer dashboard: pipeline map, live waveform and spectrum, event cards (damage type, frequency, position, severity), pipe material, repair recommendation with estimated quantity, rover and robot status, history and CSV export.
Risk heat-map: combines event history, pipe age and material into a colour map so authorities fix the riskiest segments first.
Citizen portal: report low gas pressure, sewage overflow, contaminated water or no supply, with photo and location. Reports get a tracking number and are routed to the right authority (WASA, gas distribution company, city corporation). Repeated reports in one area automatically raise priority and cross-check sensor alerts.
Authority dashboard: inbox, assignment, status updates, and response-time tracking.
Integration: an adapter layer sends notifications to authorities. Real integration with WASA or gas companies requires official agreements and is a pilot-stage task.
8. Example scenario (water main in Dhaka)
At night, DAS detects a continuous broadband signal 240 m along a PVC water line. The platform classifies it as a small leak and, because nearby residents report low pressure, raises the priority. The rover arrives, pinpoints the leak within a metre, marks it and photographs it. The arm applies a superabsorbent wrap with an elastic sleeve as a quick seal, and a crew schedules a small excavation for the lasting repair the next day. DAS confirms the leak signature has gone, and the platform stores the record and shows the savings.
9. Business plan
Value proposition. Find leaks earlier, fix many of them without digging a long trench, keep workers out of dangerous spaces, and prove every repair with data.
Customers
B2G (government and public utilities): water and sewerage authorities (Dhaka, Chattogram, Khulna and Rajshahi WASA), gas distribution companies (Titas, Bakhrabad, Jalalabad, Karnaphuli, Paschimanchal, Sundarban), city corporations, and local government engineering bodies.
B2B (industry and property): industrial parks and EPZs, garment and textile factories with their own water and effluent pipelines, large housing developers, power plants, fertiliser factories, and pipeline contractors.
Revenue streams
Monitoring-as-a-service: a fee per kilometre per month for DAS monitoring, dashboard and alerts.
Repair-as-a-service: a fee per verified repair using the rover or in-pipe robot.
Consumables: sealant cartridges, wraps and nozzles.
Equipment leasing: rover and robot hire for utilities that do not want to own hardware.
Performance contracts (B2G): a share of verified savings, for example, recovered water at the tariff rate.
Analytics and reporting: risk maps and audit reports for planning and loan applications.
Training and certification: for utility crews on confined-space safety and the repair kits.
Go-to-market
Pilot in one WASA zone, one industrial park,k or one factory network, with measured results.
Case study with verified savings and safety data.
Tender and framework contracts through public procurement for B2G, and direct contracts for B2B.
Partnerships: local universities for validation, contractors for installation, and development-partner-funded water and sanitation programmes.
Compliance. Potable-contact certification for any sealant that touches drinking water, explosion-protection standards for anything near gas, confined-space safety procedures, and data protection for citizen reports.
Competitive position. Inspection robots and DAS systems exist internationally and are expensive. PipeGuard BD's edge is the integrated chain (sense, locate, repair, verify, and the citizen link), designed for local pipes, local costs, and local procurement.
