13. Risks and limitations
Technical: DAS needs fibre or retrofit nodes and calibration; sealants suit small defects,s and some are temporary; surface repair depends on access points.
Safety: gas lines and confined spaces need certified procedures and equipment.
Regulatory: potable-water contact and explosion-protection certification.
Commercial: slow public procurement, late payments, and the need to prove savings.
Data: national loss figures include non-leak losses, so projections must use measured leak data from the pilot.
14. Conclusion
Pipelines in Bangladesh lose water and gas, endanger workers and disrupt cities, and the usual response is to find problems late and dig big holes. PipeGuard BD links early detection, material-specific repair, a ground-level response rover, an in-pipe robot, and a citizen reporting channel into one verified workflow. The strongest next step is a small pilot that measures real detection accuracy, sealing performance,e and cost per repair, because those numbers will turn the planning assumptions above into a fundable business case.
